explanations.E0043:
# E0043: expression without a value

An expression whose value is used must have one. A call of a function that returns nothing has none, and neither has an `if` without an `else`. An expression that never finishes, such as an `if` whose branches all `return`, has none either. Nor has a bare `break` in a `loop` whose other `break`s give it a value.

For a string literal, the error underlines the first part of the literal that never finishes.

## Why

A `let`, an argument, an operand or a condition needs a value to work with. Nothing is left to bind when the expression returns nothing, or never returns.

## Broken

```sisu
fn greet() {
    print(1)
}

fn main() {
    let x = greet()
    print(x)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn one() -> i64 {
    1
}

fn main() {
    let x = one()
    print(x)
}
```

## Related

- [W0001](W0001.md): a statement that follows one that never finishes.

explanations.E0053:
# E0053: call with the wrong number of arguments

A call passes one argument for each parameter of the function or method, and a variant's constructor one for each field of its payload: `Expr.Num(1)`. The array methods `len()` and `sum()` take none and `contains(x)` takes one; a conversion `i64(...)` or `u8(...)` takes one, and so do `panic(...)` and `todo(...)`; `read_line()` takes none.

The error names a method as the receiver's type and the method: `i64.min`, `f64.sqrt`, `string.len`, `[i64].contains`, `Box<i64>.get`. The type is the one that the call has. It is not the type that the `extend` block writes. A call on a `[U]` names `[U].contains`, not `[T].contains`. A call on a type parameter through a bound names the interface of the bound: `Show.show`. A function has no receiver, and the error names it alone: `add`.

## Why

Sisu has no default values for parameters and no functions that take a varying number of arguments. Each parameter needs its value, and a value with no parameter has nowhere to go.

## Broken

```sisu
fn add(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {
    a + b
}

fn main() {
    print(add(1))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn add(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {
    a + b
}

fn main() {
    print(add(1, 2))
}
```

## Related

- [E0064](E0064.md): a constructor with more arguments than the class or variant has fields.

explanations.E0055:
# E0055: labeled argument in a call

Only a constructor takes labels on its arguments: `Point(x: 1)`. A function call, a method call and `print` take plain arguments in parameter order. A variant takes labels only when it declares them, `Shape.Rect(w: 2, h: 3)`; a variant that declares none, `Expr.Num(2)`, takes plain arguments too.

## Why

Labels on the arguments of function calls are planned. Until they arrive, a label there is reported, not ignored, so a program does not rely on a meaning that has not arrived. On a variant, the labels say which payload field each argument fills, so a variant without them has nothing to match a label against. The fix there removes the labels, but as an unsafe fix: a label may name the field you meant to declare.

## Broken

```sisu
fn double(n: i64) -> i64 {
    n * 2
}

fn main() {
    print(double(n: 2))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn double(n: i64) -> i64 {
    n * 2
}

fn main() {
    print(double(2))
}
```

explanations.E0056:
# E0056: constructor without every field

A constructor names every field of its class, in declaration order: `Point(x: 1, y: 2)`. A variant with a labeled payload is built the same way: `Shape.Rect(w: 2, h: 3)`.

## Why

Sisu has no default values for fields. Every field of a new object or enum value needs a value, so a constructor that leaves one out cannot build it.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
    y: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.x + p.y)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
    y: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    print(p.x + p.y)
}
```

## Related

- [E0064](E0064.md): a constructor with more arguments than the class or variant has fields.
- [E0066](E0066.md): a constructor that names its fields out of order.

explanations.E0059:
# E0059: `print`, `eprint` or `dbg` of something it cannot print

`print` and `eprint` take exactly one value, and write it on its own line: `print` to standard output, `eprint` to standard error. `dbg` takes exactly one value too: it writes the value to standard error with its position and source text, and returns it. The message names the function that was called, and it says which rule the call breaks.

## Why

A call that returns nothing has no value to print: the message says that its argument has no value. A call with no argument, or with several, is not a call with one value: the message says that the function takes one value. The rules for `dbg` are the rules for `print`: `dbg(greet())` of a call that returns nothing, `dbg()` and `dbg(1, 2)` are all this error.

## Broken

```sisu
fn greet() {
    print(1)
}

fn main() {
    print(greet())
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn greet() -> string {
    "hello"
}

fn main() {
    print(greet())
}
```

## Related

- [W0015](W0015.md): `print` or `eprint` of an optional.

explanations.E0062:
# E0062: optional used as if it were present

A field read, a method call, an index or a `for` needs a value, not an optional of one. An optional may be `None`, which has no fields, methods or elements.

## Why

Sisu has no null pointer errors at runtime: the checker makes the program say what happens when the optional is `None`. `?.` reads the member only when the optional holds a value, and gives `None` otherwise. In an assignment or a `push`, `?.` makes the change only when the optional holds a value, and does nothing otherwise. `if let` runs a block with the value only when it is present. `let ... else` and `?? return` unwrap the value and leave when it is `None`.

The checker first looks for the member on the value inside the optional, as `?.` does. When that value has no such member, as in `n.size()` on a `Node?` where `Node` has no `size`, or the member does not apply to it, the error is the one that `?.` gives, such as [E0063](E0063.md) or [E0058](E0058.md), not E0062: a `?.` would not fix it.

An assignment to an element, such as `m?.items[0] = 2`, writes into the array that `m?.items` holds. An `if let` of that array binds a copy, and you cannot write to a copy. The help names the object of the `?.` instead: `if let m = m { m.items[0] = 2 }` writes into the array of `m`.

A `push` onto an optional array, such as `xs.push(2)` on an `[i64]?`, gets no `if let` help, because an unwrapped array is a copy. The `?.` fix is the form that changes the array: `xs?.push(2)`. The help names the object of a `?.` only when that object stores the array, as in `bag?.items.push(4)`.

An optional of an optional, such as a `Node??`, has two layers, and `?.` takes only one off: `n?.value` on a `Node??` reads `value` from a `Node?`, which may be `None` too. Peel the other layer first with `??`: `(n ?? None)?.value`.

## Broken

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
    next: Node?
}

fn second(n: Node) -> i64 {
    n.next.value
}

fn main() {
    print(second(Node(value: 1, next: Node(value: 2, next: None))))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
    next: Node?
}

fn second(n: Node) -> i64 {
    if let next = n.next {
        next.value
    } else {
        0
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(second(Node(value: 1, next: Node(value: 2, next: None))))
}
```

## Related

- [E0049](E0049.md): `?.` on a value that is not optional.

explanations.E0063:
# E0063: field or method that does not exist

A field read or a method call must name a member of the receiver's type: a field or method of its class, the array methods `len()`, `sum()` and `contains(x)`, the `string` methods `len()`, `chars()`, `bytes()` and `words()`, or the methods of `f64` and `i64`, such as `x.sqrt()` and `n.max(m)`. A built-in type's name takes a constant: `f64.pi`, `f64.tau`, `f64.e`, `f64.infinity`, `f64.nan`, `f64.max`, `f64.min_positive` and `f64.epsilon`. No other type has any, so `i64.max` is this error too. A method of `f64` is no method of `i64`: `n.sqrt()` with an `i64` `n` is this error too. A constructor's labels must name fields of its class, or of the labeled payload of its variant. The methods of an enum count as methods of its type, but its value has no fields: that is [E0121](E0121.md).

When a member of the receiver's type has a similar name, the error offers it as an unsafe fix. The unsafe fix may be wrong: the name could be a member that was never written. For `let x = 2.5` and `x.sqrtt()`, the unsafe fix is `sqrt`; for `f64.PI`, it is `pi`.

The same error is reported for a method call on a value of a type parameter: the type parameter has only the methods of its bounds. When exactly one interface of the program declares a method of that name, and it takes no type parameters, the help adds the bound: for `x.area()` on a value of type `T`, `add a bound: T: Shape`. If `T` has bounds already, the help lists them with the new one.

## Why

Sisu does not resolve a misspelled member name on its own. A misspelled name would otherwise read some other value or run some other code.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.y)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.x)
}
```

explanations.E0064:
# E0064: constructor with more arguments than fields

A constructor names every field of its class once, in declaration order: `Point(x: 1)` for a class with the one field `x`. A variant's constructor does the same for its payload: `Shape.Rect(w: 2, h: 3)` for a variant with two labeled fields.

## Why

Each argument of a constructor sets one field. An argument after the last field has no field to set.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    print(p.x)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
    y: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    print(p.x + p.y)
}
```

## Related

- [E0056](E0056.md): a constructor without every field.

explanations.E0065:
# E0065: constructor argument without a label

Each argument of a constructor is labeled with the field it sets: `Point(x: 1)`. A variant whose payload is labeled is built the same way: `Shape.Rect(w: 2, h: 3)`. A variant with an unlabeled payload, such as `Num(i64)`, takes its arguments without labels; a label there is [E0055](E0055.md).

## Why

The label says which field gets which value, so a constructor reads the same however many fields its class or variant has, and swapping two values of one type cannot go unnoticed.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(1)
    print(p.x)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.x)
}
```

explanations.E0124:
# E0124: `match` on a type it cannot take

`match` takes an enum, an optional, an integer, a `bool`, a `char` or a `string`. A class, an array or an `f64` is not one of them.

## Why

A pattern names a variant or a value that the program can compare exactly. A class has no variants, and an `f64` does not compare exactly. Read the class's fields.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
    y: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    let n = match p {
        _ => 0
    }
    print(n)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
    y: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    let n = match p.x {
        1 => p.y
        _ => 0
    }
    print(n)
}
```

explanations.E0130:
# E0130: `match` that does not cover every value

A `match` needs an arm for every value its scrutinee can hold. When a value reaches no arm, the error names one such value as a pattern, at the `match` keyword: "`match` does not cover `Mul(_, _)`". When an arm has the missing value's variant at the top, a label on that arm says which part of the variant it covers: "`Add(Num(_), _)` covers only `Num(_), _`". When a guarded arm may not match the value, a label on the `match` keyword says so: a guarded arm covers nothing, since its guard can be false.

Integers and `char`s are finite, so literals and ranges that together hold every value cover the type: `0..=127` and `128..=255` cover a `u8`. A missing integer or `char` is named as the first gap from the bottom, "`match` does not cover `10..=255`", with the help "add `_ =>`"; so is a `string`, which only `_` or a name covers.

An optional has two values to cover: `None`, and `Some` of each value inside. A missing value of an optional is named as a pattern at the optional's position: "`match` does not cover `None`", "`match` does not cover `Some(None)`" in an optional of an optional, or the missing variant of the enum inside, "`match` does not cover `Square(_)`".

## Why

A `match` is an expression, so it must produce a value, or leave, for every input. Checking coverage when the program compiles turns a forgotten variant into an error at the `match`, instead of a crash when that value arrives. Rust and Swift reject such a `match` too. Adding a variant to an enum then points at every `match` that must handle it.

The unsafe fix adds `_ => todo` as the last arm. It is unsafe because a catch-all also takes every variant added later, which then goes unnoticed.

## Broken

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
    Add(Expr, Expr)
    Mul(Expr, Expr)
}

fn eval(e: Expr) -> i64 {
    match e {
        Num(n) => n
        Add(a, b) => eval(a) + eval(b)
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(eval(Expr.Add(Expr.Num(1), Expr.Num(2))))
    print(eval(Expr.Mul(Expr.Num(2), Expr.Num(3))))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
    Add(Expr, Expr)
    Mul(Expr, Expr)
}

fn eval(e: Expr) -> i64 {
    match e {
        Num(n) => n
        Add(a, b) => eval(a) + eval(b)
        Mul(a, b) => eval(a) * eval(b)
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(eval(Expr.Add(Expr.Num(1), Expr.Num(2))))
    print(eval(Expr.Mul(Expr.Num(2), Expr.Num(3))))
}
```

## Related

- [W0006](W0006.md): an arm that no value reaches.

explanations.E0138:
# E0138: type that cannot be a type argument

A type argument is a type a value can have: not `unit` and not `never`. An optional, such as `i64?`, can be a type argument.

## Why

A type parameter stands for the type of values: of a field, a payload, a parameter or an element. `unit` and `never` are the types of no value, so they stand for nothing there.

## Broken

```sisu
class Cell<T> {
    value: T
}

fn main() {
    let c: Cell<unit>? = None
    print(c == None)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Cell<T> {
    value: T
}

fn main() {
    let c: Cell<bool>? = Cell(value: true)
    print(c?.value ?? false)
}
```

## Related

- [E0137](E0137.md): type arguments that do not fit.

explanations.E0154:
# E0154: type that does not conform to an interface that is needed

A type must conform to an interface when another interface inherits it, or when a bound asks for it. A bound that names a parameter of a type alias is checked at each use of the alias, on the type that the use names: with `class Heap<T: Ord>`, `type H<T> = Heap<T>` is clean, and `H<bool>` is this error at the use. So is `X<string>` for `type X<T> = T.Item`, which needs `T` to conform to the interface that declares `Item`. An interface can inherit others: `interface Shape: Named` asks each type that conforms to `Shape` to conform to `Named` too. A conformance in the list of a class or an enum meets the inherited interfaces with the methods of its body. An `extend Square: Shape` block holds the methods of `Shape` only, so the inherited interface needs a conformance of its own.

## Why

A function that takes a `Shape` can use every requirement of `Named` on it. The type must conform to `Named` for that to work. Add a conformance to the inherited interface: in the list of the class, or in an `extend Square: Named` block.

A value converts to `any Shape` where its type conforms to `Shape`. When the type does not, this error is at the value, and a note shows the `any Shape` that asks for the conformance. Write the conformance for the type, or use a value of a type that has it.

## Broken

```sisu
interface Named {
    fn name(self) -> string
}

interface Shape: Named {
    fn area(self) -> f64
}

class Square {
    side: f64
}

extend Square: Shape {
    fn area(self) -> f64 {
        self.side * self.side
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(Square(side: 2.0).area())
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
interface Named {
    fn name(self) -> string
}

interface Shape: Named {
    fn area(self) -> f64
}

class Square {
    side: f64
}

extend Square: Named {
    fn name(self) -> string {
        "square"
    }
}

extend Square: Shape {
    fn area(self) -> f64 {
        self.side * self.side
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(Square(side: 2.0).area())
}
```

## Bounds

A bound `T: Shape` asks each type argument for `T` to conform to `Shape`. The compiler checks the bound where a type argument meets it: in a call of a generic function or method, in a construction of a class or a variant, and in each type that names a class or an enum with a bound, such as the type of a local, a parameter, a field or a payload. The error points at the argument or the type that fails, and its second label shows the bound. A call that fails gives one error, and the callee's body adds none.

When the failing type is a type parameter of the function that you write, add the bound to it. The help shows the parameter with the bounds that it has, and the new one: `add a bound: T: Named & Same`. The help is not shown for an interface that takes type parameters.

When the failing type is a class or an enum with no conformance, the help says to make it conform: `make Node conform to Eq`. There is no such help for `AnyObject`, because only classes conform to it, and its label says so. For a bound of a function, a class or an enum that your program writes, the label names the part when a part of the type fails, as `Node` in `[Node]`: "`[Node]` holds `Node`, which does not conform to `Eq`", and the help is for the part. For a bound of an `extend` block or of the prelude, the label names the method and the bound of the call instead.

A bound of a generic interface asks for its type arguments too. A type that conforms to `Convert<Fahrenheit>` does not meet the bound `T: Convert<Kelvin>`. The error names the interface with the arguments of the bound. Add a conformance with those arguments.

An interface can bound its own type parameters: with `interface Feed<T: Same>`, each type argument for `T` must conform to `Same`. The compiler checks this wherever a program writes the interface with arguments: in the list of a class or an enum, in an `extend` block, in a bound, and in the list of an interface that inherits it. A conformance that comes later in the program counts. When the argument is a type parameter, as in `interface Relay<T>: Feed<T>`, give the parameter the bound: `interface Relay<T: Same>: Feed<T>`.

## Associated types

An interface can give its associated type bounds: with `type Item: Same`, the type that each conformance chooses for `Item` must conform to `Same`. The error points at the chosen type, and its second label shows the bound. An argument of a call that is a type of `S.Item` gets its type from the conformance of `S`, so a call whose `S` does not conform to the interface gives this error once, at the argument that bound `S`. The argument that waits for `S.Item` adds no error of its own.

## Conditional conformance

A block with bounds on its type parameters gives a conformance only to the types whose arguments meet the bounds. With `extend<T: Same> Pair<T>: Same`, a `Pair<Circle>` conforms to `Same` when `Circle` does, and a `Pair<Node>` does not when `Node` does not. A call that needs the conformance, or a call of a method of the block, reports the type that fails. The second label of the error shows the bound of the function or the block that asks for it, and a third shows the bound of the conformance that the type fails.

A conformance of such a block asks for the inherited interfaces under the same bounds. With `extend<T: Same> Heap<T>: Ord2` and `Ord2: Same`, a `Heap<T>` needs a conformance to `Same` for every `T: Same`. When the type has none, the help writes the block to add. When it has one that needs more than the block gives, such as `extend<T: Ord2> Heap<T>: Same`, a label shows the bound that the block does not give. The help names the bound to add or the part to make conform, as for a use: `add a bound: T: Same & Ord2`. It comes only when each bound that fails needs the same change. It does not write a second block, which would conform twice.

The list of a class or an enum asks the same of a block that gives it an inherited interface. With `class Stack<T>: Ord2`, `Ord2: Same` and `extend<T: Ord2> Stack<T>: Same`, the `Stack<T>` of the list has no bound on `T`, so the block does not hold for it. The error points at `Ord2` in the list, and a label shows the bound that the list does not give. Give the parameter of the class the bound, `class Stack<T: Ord2>: Ord2`, or write the conformance in the class.

## Language interfaces

`Display`, `Eq`, `Ord` and `AnyObject` are in the prelude, and a bound can name them. The compiler writes the conformance of many types (`i64`, `string`, an array of numbers, an enum of numbers), as the page for [E0152](E0152.md) says. A class has no `Eq` and no `Ord`, and an enum has no `Ord`; the help for a bound writes the interfaces that the type parameter needs: `add a bound: T: Eq & Ord`. When `Ord` is needed but `Eq` is not given, the type needs a conformance to `Eq` too, because `Ord` inherits it.

Only a class conforms to `AnyObject`. A bound `T: AnyObject` that a number, an enum or an array fails is this error, with the note "only classes conform to `AnyObject`". A type that is not a class cannot list `AnyObject`; that is [E0158](E0158.md).

## `contains`

The prelude writes `contains` for an array whose element type conforms to `Eq`, as the block `extend<T: Eq> [T]`. A call on an array of another element type is this error, at the call, and it names the element type. The label names the method and the bound that the call needs. It writes the element type that your program has, not the `T` of the prelude. For a `[Node]`, the label is "`contains` needs `Node: Eq`". For a `[U]`, it is "`contains` needs `U: Eq`". When the element holds a type that has no conformance, the label names that type too. Then the label is "`contains` needs `Node?: Eq`, and `Node` does not conform to `Eq`". No label points into the prelude.

An element that is a class with no written `Eq` gives "`Node` does not conform to `Eq`" for `nodes.contains(n)` on a `[Node]`, and the help says to make `Node` conform to `Eq`. Do that, or look for the element with `is`. The help is the same for an array of arrays of `Node`, an optional `Node` and an enum with a `Node` in a case: the label names `Node`.

An element can also be a type with a written conformance whose bound fails. With `extend<A: Eq> Pair<A>: Eq`, a `Pair<Node>` does not conform to `Eq`, and a label shows the bound `A: Eq`. The help is for the part inside the type that fails: `make Node conform to Eq`. For a `Pair<T>`, it is `add a bound: T: Eq`. The help comes only when each bound that fails needs the same change: with `extend<A: Eq, B: Ord> Two<A, B>: Eq`, a `Two<T, bool>` gets none. A part that no help can fix, such as a `bool` that must conform to `Ord`, gets none.

An element of a type parameter `T` gives "`T` does not conform to `Eq`" for `xs.contains(x)` on a `[T]`, and the help shows the bound to add: `T: Eq`. The same holds for an element of `T?`, as in a `[T?]`.

An argument that is not an element is the mistake first: it is [E0032](E0032.md), and a conformance to `Eq` would not make the program right.

## Related

- [E0152](E0152.md): a conformance that a type does not meet.
- [E0158](E0158.md): a conformance to `AnyObject` of a type that is not a class.

explanations.W0003:
# W0003: `todo` left in the program

A `todo` marks code that is not written yet. It panics when it runs, with the message `not yet implemented`, or `not yet implemented: <message>` for `todo("message")`. The warning points at each `todo`, and the program still builds.

## Why

A `todo` lets a function type-check before its body exists: it is an expression of type `never`, so it stands in for any value. A program that still holds one builds, but it should not ship that way, so the compiler says where each one is. Rust's `todo!()` does not warn, and Clippy's `todo` lint is off by default; Sisu warns because the next run that reaches the `todo` will fail.

## Broken

```sisu
fn area(width: i64, height: i64) -> i64 {
    todo("multiply")
}

fn main() {
    print(area(2, 3))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn area(width: i64, height: i64) -> i64 {
    width * height
}

fn main() {
    print(area(2, 3))
}
```
